# Introduction to Graphs {#sec-gr}

::: {.callout-note title="Sources"}
**`graph_book` ch. 1** (five lessons, five patterns) · **CLRS** §22.1 (representations) · **CPH** ch. 11–12 · **CP-Algorithms** *Graph representations*, *Depth-first search*, *Breadth-first search*.

**Coverage:** vertices and edges, simple and multi graphs, the handshake · walks, paths, cycles, components, trees, bipartite graphs, functional graphs · adjacency lists, matrices, and implicit neighbours · the first depth-first and breadth-first walks · a graph model written down before the code · degrees, reachability, grid flood fills, shortest paths on an implicit state graph, and cycles in a graph where every vertex has one outgoing arc.
:::

::: {.callout-tip title="What you will be able to do"}
- Read a drawing as a set of pairs, and store those pairs so a later search is linear in the input.
- Count degrees, and answer a question that is only a formula on those counts.
- Mark a vertex when it is discovered, and know which vertices a source can reach.
- Treat a grid cell, a number, or a lock code as a vertex whose neighbours are computed, not stored.
- Follow a functional graph until the walk repeats, and read the cycle off the timestamps.
:::


## Why this matters

A graph is the right model whenever the input is "things, and a relation between them." A road joins two cities. A trust statement points from one person to another. A grid cell touches the cell beside it. A button press turns one number into another. Once the relation is a set of pairs, the same four tools answer most of the questions in this chapter: a degree counter, a search that marks vertices as they are discovered, a queue that measures distance in edges, and a walk that stops at the first repeated vertex.

The drawing on a whiteboard is a picture of the graph. The graph itself is the pair $(V, E)$. Two drawings of the same pairs are the same graph.

## Vertices and edges {#sec-gr-vert}

$V$ is the set of vertices, $E$ the set of edges. An undirected edge $\{u, v\}$ is stored as the two arcs $(u, v)$ and $(v, u)$, because a walk may cross it either way. A directed edge is one arc. The phrases "u trusts v", "u points to v", "from room u you can enter room v", and "$a_i$ is next after $i$" are all arcs $u \to v$.

A weight lives on the edge. The path with the fewest edges and the path with the smallest total weight can be different paths. On the sample below, $S \to B \to T$ uses two edges and weight $6$. $S \to A \to B \to T$ uses three edges and weight $4$.

::: {#gr-weight .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
W = {("S", "A"): 2, ("S", "B"): 5, ("A", "T"): 7, ("B", "T"): 1, ("A", "B"): 1}
hop = W[("S", "B")] + W[("B", "T")]
cheap = W[("S", "A")] + W[("A", "B")] + W[("B", "T")]
assert (hop, cheap) == (6, 4)
```
