:::

**Worked trace.** Example 2 has degrees $2, 3, 3, 3, 1$. Every pair among cities $1, 2, 3$ is a road, so each of those pairs scores $3 + 3 - 1 = 5$. City $4$ has degree $1$, and pairing it with a degree-$3$ city scores $4$.

**Edge cases.** Two cities and no road: rank $0$. One road: rank $1$. The complete graph $K_4$ has degree $3$ and every pair joined, so the rank is $5$. $K_{100}$ has degree $99$ and rank $197$.

**Pitfalls.** Cities are $0..n-1$, so the degree array has length $n$. Subtracting the shared road on a pair that is not an edge drops a correct $5$ to $4$. Adding the road twice, once in each degree and again as a bonus, inflates the first sample to $5$.

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Forgetting the shared road.** $\deg(u) + \deg(v)$ on the first sample is $5$. The rank is $4$.
- **The two largest degrees, and stop.** On a 4-cycle every degree is $2$. The adjacent pair scores $3$ and the opposite pair scores $4$. A single pair of maximum-degree cities can be the adjacent one.
- **Assuming the graph is connected.** The rank is defined for every pair. A search that stays in city $0$'s component misses the pair in the other component of the third sample.
- **One direction in the matrix.** A road listed as $[v, u]$ is invisible to a later lookup of $(u, v)$ unless both entries are set.
:::

**Variants.** [LC 2497 · Maximum Star Sum of a Graph](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-star-sum-of-a-graph/){difficulty="Medium"} is local to one centre: keep the positive neighbour values and take at most $k$ of them. [LC 2285 · Maximum Total Importance of Roads](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-total-importance-of-roads/){difficulty="Medium"} rewrites the edge sum as $\sum_v \mathrm{value}(v) \cdot \deg(v)$, so the largest value goes to the largest degree. On a tree, the number of components after deleting two vertices is this rank shifted by a constant.

Source: [LC 1615 · Maximal Network Rank](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximal-network-rank/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}


## Pattern 2: Reachability {#sec-gr-p2}

**From counting edges to following them.** Degrees answer questions about one vertex at a time. Whether $t$ can be reached from $s$ depends on whole paths, so we walk: mark a vertex when it is first pushed, and stop when nothing new can be reached.

**Template.** Build the adjacency lists. From the source, push a vertex only when it is still unmarked, and mark it at that moment. The search ends when the stack or queue is empty, or when the target is popped.

**Recognition.** "Can you get from $s$ to $t$?", "which vertices are reachable from $s$?", "does every vertex get visited if we start at $0$?". The edges may be directed. Direction decides which way the list is filled.

**Cost.** $O(n + m)$ time and memory. A matrix is the wrong store at $n \le 2 \cdot 10^5$. A recursive search is the wrong control flow on a path of that length.

::: {.callout-important title="Pattern template"}
Mark on discovery. An undirected edge is stored twice. A directed edge is stored once, in the direction the statement allows you to walk.
:::

### Problem: Find if Path Exists in Graph {#sec-gr-p2-path}

Undirected graph, $n$ vertices labelled $0..n-1$, edges `edges[i] = [u, v]`. Return whether `source` can reach `destination`.

**Graph model.** Undirected. Both directions go into the lists. $n \le 2 \cdot 10^5$, so the search is a queue or an explicit stack, and the store is lists.

**Invariants.** `source == destination` is true before any edge is read: the trivial walk has length $0$. A vertex enters the queue at most once.

**Approaches.**

1. **Breadth-first search** — mark on push, return when the destination is popped. $O(n + m)$.
2. **Disjoint sets** — union every edge, then compare the roots of the two endpoints. Same asymptotics, and it answers every pair once the unions are done. The chapter on disjoint sets develops the structure; the form here is only the connectivity test.

