:::

**Worked trace.** The official grid has a land component of size $6$ in the right-centre, and smaller components elsewhere. The four corner-touching lands of a diagonal pair stay separate. Under 8-connectivity that component and its diagonal neighbours would merge further; the problem's answer $6$ is the 4-connected one.

**Edge cases.** All water returns $0$. A single land cell returns $1$. The $2 \times 2$ block returns $4$. The mark-on-pop walk of that block enqueues the last cell twice and returns $5$.

**Pitfalls.** Eight directions on the official grid join lands that only meet at a corner and produce a larger number. Writing the $0$ after the recursive calls recounts a cell through a cycle of calls and overflows the stack. The constraints $R, C \le 50$ are what make the recursive sink safe.

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Sinking after the recursive calls.** The neighbour still sees the cell as land and walks back in. Write the $0$ before the four calls.
- **No island.** `best` starts at $0$. An all-water grid is $0$, which a sentinel below every area will not return.
- **Diagonal neighbours.** The statement is horizontal or vertical. Eight directions join lands that only meet at a corner and produce a larger number on the official grid.
- **A counter that is not cleared.** An area kept across islands adds the previous island into the next one. The total amount of land comes back. Returning the area from the fill makes that mistake impossible.
:::

**Variants.** Turning one water cell into land and joining the distinct islands it touches is [LC 827 · Making A Large Island](https://leetcode.com/problems/making-a-large-island/){difficulty="Hard"}. The same fill with a sum of cell values, instead of a count of cells, is [LC 2658 · Maximum Number of Fish in a Grid](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-number-of-fish-in-a-grid/){difficulty="Medium"}. The boundary, rather than the area, is [LC 463 · Island Perimeter](https://leetcode.com/problems/island-perimeter/){difficulty="Easy"}, and it needs no search. Eight-connected land uses eight calls; the corner-touching cells of the official grid then become one island.

Source: [LC 695 · Max Area of Island](https://leetcode.com/problems/max-area-of-island/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}


## Pattern 4: Implicit state graphs {#sec-gr-p4}

**From cells to any state.** A grid cell is one kind of state. Any situation with legal moves is a graph whose vertices are generated as you go, and breadth-first search gives the fewest moves.

**Template.** A state is a vertex. A legal move is an edge, computed from the state, never stored up front. Breadth-first search gives the fewest moves. The state space is bounded before the queue is allocated: every state the search is willing to push has an index in a range you chose from the constraints.

**Recognition.** The input is a number, a tuple, or a short string, and the output is a distance under a handful of edits. Building an adjacency list of "all pairs that differ by one move" is the same search with a huge constant.

**Cost.** $O(S \cdot b)$ time, where $S$ is the number of states you allow and $b$ is the number of moves from one state. For the lock, $S = 10^4$ and $b = 8$.

::: {.callout-important title="Pattern template"}
Generate the neighbours inside the search. Set the distance of the start to $0$ before the loop. Reject a neighbour that falls outside the bound you proved.
:::

### Problem: Two Buttons {#sec-gr-p4-buttons}

The integer $n$ is on the screen. One press replaces $x$ by $2x$, or by $x - 1$ when $x \ge 1$. Both $n$ and the target $m$ lie in $1..10^4$. Print the fewest presses.

**Graph model.** A vertex is an integer in $1..2\max(n, m)$. The two moves are the edges. The bound is twice the larger endpoint: the sample $3 \to 5$ walks through $6$, so a window that stops at $\max(n, m)$ never sees the target.

**Invariants.** Breadth-first search pops states in order of distance. The reverse walk is also shortest: while $m > n$, an odd $m$ undoes a decrement (replace $m$ by $m + 1$) and an even $m$ undoes a doubling (replace $m$ by $m / 2$); then add the remaining gap $n - m$.

**Approaches.**

1. **Breadth-first search** — window $2\max(n, m)$. $O(\max(n, m))$.
2. **Reverse walk** — the loop above. $O(\log m)$. It agrees with the search on every pair this chapter tests, and it is the form that stays comfortable if the limits grow.

