:::

**Worked trace.** `[3, 3, 4, 2, 3]` enters the cycle at vertex $2$: the successors are $2 \to 4 \to 3 \to 2$, length $3$. `[1, 0, 3, 1]` is a $2$-cycle on $\{0, 1\}$ plus the tail $2 \to 3 \to 1$. Processing $0$ first stamps the cycle. The later walk from $2$ hits a stamp that is older than the walk. Keeping that hit produces length $3$. Rejecting an old stamp leaves the answer at $2$.

**Edge cases.** A single vertex with no arc returns $-1$. A $2$-cycle returns $2$. A chain into a missing arc returns $-1$. The best value starts at $-1$, so "no cycle" and "a cycle of length $0$" are different answers, and only the first occurs.

**Pitfalls.** Reading `when[v]` when `v` is $-1$ is an out-of-range access; the test is `v != -1` first. A boolean "visited" array has no start time, so a tail that falls into a cycle found earlier looks like a new cycle. Initialising the answer to $0$ turns the acyclic sample into a wrong zero.

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **A boolean visited array.** It cannot tell this walk from an earlier component. On `[1, 0, 3, 1]` the tail into the old cycle reports length $3$. The test `when[v] >= start` leaves the answer at $2$.
- **A fresh search from every vertex.** One long chain into a cycle makes that quadratic. One `when` array measures every cycle in linear time.
- **The tail counted into the length.** The length is `time - when[v]` at the repeated vertex. The walk from $0$ on `[3, 3, 4, 2, 3]` is longer than the cycle, and the cycle has length $3$.
- **Returning $0$.** A graph with no cycle returns $-1$. Every real cycle has length at least $2$, so $-1$ is not an answer that a cycle can produce.
:::

**Variants.** A permutation has no tails and no missing arc, so a plain seen array measures every cycle: [LC 565 · Array Nesting](https://leetcode.com/problems/array-nesting/){difficulty="Medium"}. The first repeated vertex of every walk is @sec-gr-p5-badge. Counting cycles, rather than measuring the longest, is the same test with a counter in place of the length. Seating people next to their favourite is [LC 2127 · Maximum Employees to Be Invited to a Meeting](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-employees-to-be-invited-to-a-meeting/){difficulty="Hard"}: one long cycle, or every 2-cycle extended by the chains into its two ends.

Source: [LC 2360 · Longest Cycle in a Graph](https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-cycle-in-a-graph/){.solved difficulty="Hard"}


[**From a stored graph to one hidden in the input.** Both problems so far may mark visited vertices. Find the Duplicate must leave the array unchanged and use $O(1)$ memory, so two pointers find the cycle's entrance without marking anything.]{.flow-bridge}


### Problem: Find the duplicate number {#sec-gr-p5-duplicate}

`nums` has length $n + 1$. Every value lies in $1..n$, and exactly one value occurs more than once. Return it. The array stays unchanged, and the extra memory is $O(1)$.

::: {.p-intuition}
Read the array as arrows $i \to \texttt{nums}[i]$. Every index has exactly one arrow out and no arrow enters $0$, so the walk from $0$ runs down a tail into a cycle. The vertex where the tail joins the cycle has two arrows in, so two indices store it: it is the duplicate. Two pointers (tortoise and hare) find that entrance in $O(n)$ time with two integers of memory.
:::

**Graph model.** The vertices are the indices $0..n$. The arc from $i$ is $i \to \texttt{nums}[i]$. Every value is a legal index, so the out-degree is exactly $1$: a functional graph stored in the input itself. Vertex $0$ has in-degree $0$, because no value is $0$. The question is the entrance of the cycle on the walk that starts at $0$.

**Invariants.** Write $x_k$ for the vertex reached by $k$ steps from $0$, and write $\mu \ge 1$ for the tail length and $\lambda \ge 1$ for the cycle length. Then $x_\mu$ is the entrance $e$, the vertices $x_0, \dots, x_{\mu+\lambda-1}$ are distinct, and $x_{k+\lambda} = x_k$ once $k \ge \mu$.

The entrance is the duplicate. It is the successor of $x_{\mu-1}$ (still on the tail) and of $x_{\mu+\lambda-1}$ (on the cycle). Those are two different indices holding the value $e$.

Phase 1 moves a tortoise one step and a hare two steps, from $0$. After $t$ rounds they stand at $x_t$ and $x_{2t}$. For $t < \mu$ the tortoise is still on the tail, a vertex that never repeats, so they differ. For $t \ge \mu$ both are on the cycle, and they meet exactly when $\lambda$ divides $t$. The first such $t^\ast$ is at most $\mu + \lambda - 1$.

Phase 2 puts the tortoise back at $0$ and moves both pointers one step. After $k$ steps they stand at $x_k$ and $x_{t^\ast+k}$. At $k = \mu$ the second index is $t^\ast + \mu$, a whole number of laps past the entrance, so both stand at $e$. For $k < \mu$ the restarted pointer is still on the tail and the other is already on the cycle. They first meet at $e$, after exactly $\mu$ steps.

**Approaches.**

1. **Tortoise and hare** — two integers. $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ memory. This is the form to submit.
2. **Counting** — binary search on the value. $\mathrm{cnt}(x) > x$ exactly when the duplicate is at most $x$. $O(n \log n)$ time, $O(1)$ memory, and no use of the arrows. The pigeonhole that makes the test monotone is @sec-pf-p4-duplicate.
3. **Brent** — the hare walks one step, and the tortoise jumps to the hare every $1, 2, 4, \dots$ steps. The gap at the meeting is $\lambda$. Advance one pointer $\lambda$ steps from $0$, then move both: they meet at the same entrance.

