[LC 565 · Array Nesting](https://leetcode.com/problems/array-nesting/){difficulty="Medium"}. `nums` is a permutation, so the graph is a disjoint union of cycles. The answer is the longest cycle. Stamp vertices as you walk, and skip a start that is already stamped. $O(n)$.

`[5, 4, 0, 3, 1, 6, 2]` has a cycle of length $4$.

::: {#265335f2 .cell execution_count=89}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def array_nesting(nums):
    seen = [False] * len(nums)
    best = 0
    for s in range(len(nums)):
        if seen[s]:
            continue
        v, length = s, 0
        while not seen[v]:
            seen[v] = True
            length += 1
            v = nums[v]
        best = max(best, length)
    return best

assert array_nesting([5, 4, 0, 3, 1, 6, 2]) == 4
assert array_nesting([0, 1, 2]) == 1
```
:::


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[M] Closest node to two starts"}
[LC 2359 · Find Closest Node to Given Two Nodes](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-closest-node-to-given-two-nodes/){difficulty="Medium"}. Out-degree at most one. Walk from each start and record distances, stopping if a vertex repeats. Among vertices reached by both, minimise the larger of the two distances, and on a tie take the smallest index.

From $0$ and $1$ on `[2, 2, 3, -1]` both walks reach vertex $2$ at distance $1$.

::: {#4b19bd64 .cell execution_count=90}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def closest(edges, a, b):
    def walk(s):
        dist, v, step = {}, s, 0
        while v != -1 and v not in dist:
            dist[v] = step
            step += 1
            v = edges[v]
        return dist

    da, db = walk(a), walk(b)
    best = None
    for v in da.keys() & db.keys():
        score = max(da[v], db[v])
        if best is None or score < best[0] or score == best[0] and v < best[1]:
            best = (score, v)
    return -1 if best is None else best[1]

assert closest([2, 2, 3, -1], 0, 1) == 2
assert closest([1, 2, -1], 0, 2) == 2
```
:::


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[M] Where the cycle begins"}
[LC 142 · Linked List Cycle II](https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle-ii/){difficulty="Medium"}. Each node has one `next` pointer, or none. The same two phases start at `head`. If the hare reaches null during phase 1, there is no cycle and the answer is null. Otherwise phase 2, with one pointer back at `head`, meets at the first node of the cycle. `head` itself may lie on the cycle, in which case the tail length is $0$ and phase 2 meets immediately.

A tail of one node meets at the second node. A list with no `next` cycle returns nothing. A cycle through the head meets at the head.

::: {#ccce32a5 .cell execution_count=91}
``` {.python .cell-code}
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val):
        self.val = val
        self.next = None

def cycle_start(head):
    slow = fast = head
    while fast and fast.next:
        slow = slow.next
        fast = fast.next.next
        if slow is fast:
            break
    else:
        return None
    slow = head
    while slow is not fast:
        slow = slow.next
        fast = fast.next
    return slow

nodes = [Node(3), Node(2), Node(0), Node(-4)]
for left, right in zip(nodes, nodes[1:]):
    left.next = right
nodes[3].next = nodes[1]
assert cycle_start(nodes[0]) is nodes[1]
lone, nxt = Node(1), Node(2)
lone.next = nxt
assert cycle_start(lone) is None
c1, c2 = Node(1), Node(2)
c1.next = c2
c2.next = c1
assert cycle_start(c1) is c1
```
:::


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[E] Happy number"}
[LC 202 · Happy Number](https://leetcode.com/problems/happy-number/){difficulty="Easy"}. The successor of $x$ is the sum of the squares of its decimal digits. The graph is functional and implicit. $1$ is a self-loop. Floyd from $n$ meets at $1$ exactly when the walk reaches that loop; every other cycle is a no. $19$ is happy. $2$ is not.

::: {#89979159 .cell execution_count=92}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def is_happy(n):
    def nxt(x):
        return sum(int(d) * int(d) for d in str(x))

    slow, fast = nxt(n), nxt(nxt(n))
    while slow != fast:
        slow, fast = nxt(slow), nxt(nxt(fast))
    return slow == 1

assert is_happy(19) and not is_happy(2)
```
:::


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[M] Circular array loop"}
[LC 457 · Circular Array Loop](https://leetcode.com/problems/circular-array-loop/){difficulty="Medium"}. The arc from $i$ is $(i + \texttt{nums}[i]) \bmod n$. A cycle counts only when its length is greater than $1$ and every step on it has the same sign as $\texttt{nums}[i]$. Floyd from each start, refusing a step whose sign flipped, then clear the explored vertices so the whole scan stays linear.

`[2, -1, 1, 2, 2]` has a forward cycle of length $3$. `[-1, 2]` does not, and neither does `[-2, 1, -1, -2, -2]`.

::: {#85fa1ac2 .cell execution_count=93}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def circular_array_loop(nums):
    n = len(nums)

    def nxt(i):
        return (i + nums[i]) % n

    seen = [False] * n
    for s in range(n):
        if seen[s]:
            continue
        sign = nums[s] > 0
        index, path, v = {}, [], s
        while v not in index and not seen[v] and (nums[v] > 0) == sign and nums[v] != 0:
            index[v] = len(path)
            path.append(v)
            v = nxt(v)
        if v in index and len(path) - index[v] > 1:
            return True
        for u in path:
            seen[u] = True
    return False

assert circular_array_loop([2, -1, 1, 2, 2]) is True
assert circular_array_loop([-1, 2]) is False
assert circular_array_loop([-2, 1, -1, -2, -2]) is False
```
:::


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="[M] Teleporter"}
[AC abc167_d · Teleporter](https://atcoder.jp/contests/abc167/tasks/abc167_d). One teleporter per town, $K \le 10^{18}$. Walk until you repeat, which splits the route into a tail and a cycle. If $K$ lands in the tail, the answer is that town. Otherwise step $(K - \text{tail length}) \bmod \text{cycle length}$ from the entrance.

Towns $3, 2, 4, 1$ and $K = 5$ land on town $4$.

::: {#e130da67 .cell execution_count=94}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def teleporter(a, k):
    first, order, v = {}, [], 1
    while v not in first:
        first[v] = len(order)
        order.append(v)
        v = a[v - 1]
    mu = first[v]
    lam = len(order) - mu
    if k < mu:
        return order[k]
    return order[mu + (k - mu) % lam]

assert teleporter([3, 2, 4, 1], 5) == 4
```
:::


