## Further reading

- [CPH — ch. 11, 12 and 16: graphs, traversal and successor graphs](https://cses.fi/book/book.pdf) — graph terminology, adjacency lists and matrices, DFS and BFS, and Floyd's cycle finding on functional graphs, all at contest level.
- [Erickson — *Algorithms*, ch. 5: Basic Graph Algorithms](https://jeffe.cs.illinois.edu/teaching/algorithms/book/05-graphs.pdf) — definitions, representations and whatever-first search, the general walk that this chapter's stack and queue versions specialise.
- [Sedgewick and Wayne — *Algorithms*, §4.1: Undirected Graphs](https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/41graph/) — the adjacency-list API, DFS and BFS with full Java code, and connected components.
- [CP-Algorithms — Breadth-first search](https://cp-algorithms.com/graph/breadth-first-search.html) — BFS with distances and path recovery, and its uses on implicit state graphs.
- [CP-Algorithms — Depth First Search](https://cp-algorithms.com/graph/depth-first-search.html) — entry and exit times, edge classification and the problems DFS solves.
- [USACO Guide — Flood Fill](https://usaco.guide/silver/flood-fill) — grids as graphs, with iterative fills that avoid deep recursion.
- [Wikipedia — Cycle detection](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cycle_detection) — Floyd's tortoise and hare and Brent's algorithm, with the $\mu$ and $\lambda$ notation used in @sec-gr-p5-duplicate.
